::PC_U04_L02_GP01::
Question ID: PC_U04_L02_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.2
Question:
Which formula finds area using two sides and the included angle?
{
~A. A=bh#This misses the factor 1/2 and does not use the included angle.
=B. A=1/2ab sin(C)#Correct. The included-angle area formula is A=1/2ab sin(C).
~C. A=a^2+b^2#This is related to the Pythagorean theorem, not triangle area.
~D. A=2pi r#This is a circle formula, not a triangle formula.
}
::PC_U04_L02_GP02::
Question ID: PC_U04_L02_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.2
Question:
A triangle has sides 6 and 10 with included angle 30 degrees. What setup finds the area?
{
~A. A=6(10)sin(30)#This omits the one-half factor.
~B. A=1/2(6)(30)sin(10)#This uses an angle as a side and a side as an angle.
~C. A=1/2(10)(30)sin(6)#This uses an angle as a side and a side as an angle.
=D. A=1/2(6)(10)sin(30)#Correct. Use one-half times the two sides times sine of the included angle.
}
::PC_U04_L02_GP03::
Question ID: PC_U04_L02_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.2
Question:
If the included angle is 90 degrees, why does A=1/2ab sin(C) match right-triangle area?
{
=A. sin(90 degrees)=1#Correct. The formula becomes A=1/2ab when sin(90 degrees)=1.
~B. cos(90 degrees)=1#cos(90 degrees)=0, and cosine is not the area factor here.
~C. sin(90 degrees)=0#This would make the area zero, which is not correct for a right triangle.
~D. The sides must be equal#The sides do not have to be equal.
}
::PC_U04_L02_GP04::
Question ID: PC_U04_L02_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.2
Question:
What does included angle mean?
{
~A. An angle outside the triangle#The included angle is inside the triangle.
~B. The largest angle only#It does not have to be the largest angle.
=C. The angle between the two known sides#Correct. The included angle is formed by the two known sides.
~D. Any angle not used#The included angle is essential in this formula.
}
::PC_U04_L02_GP05::
Question ID: PC_U04_L02_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.2
Question:
A triangle has sides 9 and 12 with included angle 30 degrees. What is the area?
{
=A. 27#Correct. A=1/2(9)(12)sin(30)=54(1/2)=27.
~B. 54#This misses the sine factor because sin(30 degrees)=1/2.
~C. 108#This multiplies the side lengths without the one-half and sine factors.
~D. 216#This greatly overmultiplies and does not use the area formula correctly.
}